# /etc/udev/rules.d/99-wendyos-config.rules
#
# Stable link /dev/wendyos/config for the /config partition on the disk / is
# mounted from.
#
# A GPT partition name is unique on one disk, not on the machine. A device that
# once ran WendyOS from another disk -- an AGX Orin moved from eMMC to NVMe --
# has a "config" partition on both, and LABEL=config or the by-partlabel link
# then names whichever one udev handled last. Mounting the old disk's /config is
# not harmless. A data.enroll.pending marker left on it would make the /data
# resolver wipe the live LUKS header on the root disk, and /config/tee would
# come from the wrong disk.
#
# The rule asks root-disk-part (data-device recipe) whether this partition is
# the one named "config" on the root disk, the same check rule 1 of
# 99-wendyos-data.rules makes for "data". See that file for why it is a udev
# SYMLINK, why the prefix is 99, and why PROGRAM is given %k and the program
# answers only yes or no.
#
# Unlike the /data rule, nothing here looks at the filesystem. On Qualcomm the
# partition is blank on first boot, and wendyos-config-init waits for this link
# before it formats the partition, so the link has to exist with no filesystem
# on it. It also keeps the link in place across the FAT32 to ext4 conversion.
#
# MBR has no partition names, so this never matches on raspberrypi3-64. That
# board keeps LABEL=config in its fstab.
#
# Consumers: the x86, VM and RPi4/5 fstabs, the /config line the image adds to
# the Tegra fstab, and the Qualcomm config.mount and wendyos-config-init.
ACTION!="remove", SUBSYSTEM=="block", ENV{ID_PART_ENTRY_SCHEME}=="gpt", \
    ENV{ID_PART_ENTRY_NAME}=="config", \
    PROGRAM="/usr/sbin/root-disk-part config %k", \
    SYMLINK+="wendyos/config"
